1 NAME Isaak /Goldschmidt/
2 GIVN Isaak
2 _MARNM Goldschmidt
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE BEF 1688
1 DEAT
2 DATE BET 1735 AND 1744
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lemgo
3 STAE North Rhine-Westphalia
3 CTRY Germany
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In 1714 Samuel Goldschmidt obtained a letter of protection (Geleitbrief) for his son Isaak in Lemgo. In 1720 Isaak and his brother Joseph Goldschmidt were appointed as Court Jews in Lippe-Detmold. In 1729 they ceas
2 CONC ed to be Court Jews (source: Heinrich Schnee, Die Hoffinanz und der Moderne Staat, Volume III (Berlin: Duncker & Humblot, 1955), p. 94).
2 CONT
2 CONT A document from Lemgo mentions Isaak, the son of Samuel Goldschmidt, in 1716. Isaak had converted his father's house in Mittelstr. 7 into a synagogue (source: Von Bürgermeistern, Handwerkern und Klosterjungfrauen. Lemgo in der Spä
2 CONC tphase der Hexenverfolgung, Ausstellung Stadtarchivs Lemgo, 2000, No. 6: Samuel Goldschmidt).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1726 the guild of the Knochenhauer in Lemgo sued Isaak Goldschmidt for illegal slaughtering (Lemgo A 3093; see also A 3683).
2 CONT
2 CONT The archives in Lemgo and Lippe mention him in Lemgo from 1703 (Lemgo A 7407) to 1735 (Lemgo A 3377). Thus, he was born in or before ca. 1688 (=1703-15). In 1744, when he published the banns for his marriage (ondertrouw) in Amster
2 CONC dam, his son Salomon declared that his parents were deceased. Thus, Isaac died between 1735 and 1744.
